\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 The use of symbolism in fiction is more difficult to learn than you might think, perhaps in large part because, when done well, symbols are almost invisible within the framework of the story. Those that aren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t invisible often feel heavy-handed or even clich\u233\'E9d, such as the inevitable use of the American flag as a symbol of inspiration in war movies. Like the flag, some symbols are almost universal, and we utilize them to evoke reader emotions without even realizing what we\u8217\'92re doing. For instance, springtime is often used to symbolize new growth, redemption, or resurrection.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 The most powerful and unique symbols are those that flow effortlessly from your story. We find a good example in the 2000 movie\~}{\f0\fs24\b0\i1 The Patriot}{\f0\fs24\b0 , directed by Roland Emmerich. After his son is murdered by a British colonel, plantation owner Benjamin Martin, played by Mel Gibson, salvages the boy\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s toy soldiers from his burning home, so he can melt the lead into musket balls. The toy soldiers appear throughout the movie, underlining the character\u8217\'92s mixed emotions of loss, grief, anger, vengeance, and eventually a desire to fight for the cause his sons believed in.}
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 The power of thematic symbols lies in their ability to drive home a point via subtlety and repetition. This is exactly what Kurt Vonnegut does so well in his revered anti-war novel\~}{\f0\fs24\b0\i1 Slaughterhouse-Five.\~}{\f0\fs24\b0 This strange novel is stripped down to stark essentials in a way that makes Vonnegut\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s repetition of thematic motifs particularly striking. He repeats certain phrases and images throughout the book, with an almost poetic variation, using both the blatant repetition and the subtle reinforcement of theme to drive his motifs deep into the reader\u8217\'92s mind. In particular, he repeatedly uses the phrases \u8220\'93blue and ivory\u8221\'94 to evoke cold, \u8220\'93mustard gas and roses\u8221\'94 to describe foul smells, and, famously, \u8220\'93so it goes\u8221\'94 to underline the tragedies referenced or described in the story.}{ }}